  "title": "How to Work with Fractions",
  "content": "<p><strong>Fractions</strong> represent parts of a whole. To <strong>add or subtract</strong> fractions, you need a <strong>common denominator</strong> — the lowest common multiple (LCM) of both denominators. Convert each fraction, then add or subtract the numerators.</p><p>To <strong>multiply</strong> fractions, multiply the numerators together and the denominators together: \\(\\frac{a}{b} \\times \\frac{c}{d} = \\frac{ac}{bd}\\). To <strong>divide</strong>, flip the second fraction and multiply: \\(\\frac{a}{b} \\div \\frac{c}{d} = \\frac{a}{b} \\times \\frac{d}{c}\\).</p><p>Always <strong>simplify</strong> your answer by dividing numerator and denominator by their highest common factor (HCF). Convert improper fractions to mixed numbers where appropriate.</p><p>For <strong>mixed numbers</strong>, convert to improper fractions before calculating. To convert: multiply the whole number by the denominator, add the numerator, and keep the same denominator.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Calculate</strong> \\(\\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{3}{4}\\)</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> LCD of 3 and 4 = 12</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Convert: \\(\\frac{8}{12} + \\frac{9}{12}\\)</p><p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Add numerators: \\(\\frac{17}{12}\\)</p><p><strong>Step 4:</strong> As a mixed number: \\(1\\frac{5}{12}\\)</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> \\(\\frac{17}{12}\\) or \\(1\\frac{5}{12}\\)</p>"
 },
